Yes, although not before turn 10000 (and even then not completely
until 20000), and it'd be cut down by the death factor (.5) so worth
less.

I want the score to mean, basically, (how much you did)*(how hard it
was to do it).  If you get to the castle wishless and get mowed down
there, it's worth more (IMO) than having gotten wishes on the way.

It might make more sense to you if you look at it as a penalty for
having used a wish.

I guess I /could/ have only given the points if some opportunity for a
wish had occurred but was not taken, but that presents two problems:
(a) this would render savefiles incompatible (something I would rather
avoid if I can) and (b) detecting a passed opportunity isn't as
straightforward as it looks.

Count only if at least one instance of a wish for 'none' is made? 
Include having picked up a wish-granting object?  What if the player
chooses to not even pick the castle wand?

